WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.190
Same question, two different stories!

2
00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.890
I asked a chatbot for a rain story

3
00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:09.730
for Primary Four, then asked again

4
00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.630
in a new chat, same words.

5
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.760
So where do its words come from?

6
00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.430
Today: how a chatbot writes,

7
00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.710
one small piece at a time.

8
00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:24.480
A chatbot doesn’t read words like we do.

9
00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.910
It cuts text into small

10
00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.630
pieces called tokens.

11
00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:32.370
A short, common word is often one token.

12
00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.450
A long or rare word, like

13
00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.910
“photosynthesis”, becomes several.

14
00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.690
Then it writes one token at a time.

15
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.090
For each, it scores every possible

16
00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.810
next token: how likely is it?

17
00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.350
It picks one, adds it, and repeats.

18
00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.940
And it doesn’t always pick the top

19
00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.480
score: a little chance is built in.

20
00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.140
Where do those scores come from?

21
00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.510
From training: the model read huge

22
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.970
amounts of text and kept guessing the

23
00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.590
next token, tuning billions of numbers

24
00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.430
until its guesses got good.

25
00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.470
It keeps patterns of language, not

26
00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:13.670
a shelf of checked facts.

27
00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:18.850
So, a large language model, or LLM, is

28
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.070
a program trained on huge amounts of

29
00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:23.390
text to predict the next token.

30
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.880
A chatbot is an LLM trained further to

31
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.560
follow instructions and chat.

32
00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:31.370
Think of a pupil who has read the

33
00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:33.830
textbook so often they can finish

34
00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:35.350
any sentence in its style.

35
00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:37.950
Ask about something they never learned,

36
00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.390
and they carry on in the same sure voice.

37
00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:43.890
Fluent, but not checked.

38
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:45.860
Quick check!

39
00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.050
A chatbot answers my question

40
00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.590
fluently and confidently.

41
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.020
What does that prove?

42
00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:56.830
Only that it sounds likely.

43
00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.220
On its own, a chatbot predicts;

44
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:01.680
it doesn’t look facts up.

45
00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.250
Some tools also search the web,

46
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:05.770
with links you can check.

47
00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.750
Every model has a knowledge cut-off,

48
00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:10.370
when its training text ends.

49
00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:13.070
And when it doesn’t know, it

50
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:13.970
can invent details.

51
00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:16.880
Language matters too.

52
00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.020
Token pieces were learned

53
00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.040
mostly from English.

54
00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.330
So the same message in Arabic, Hausa or

55
00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.750
Yoruba often needs more tokens,

56
00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.690
sometimes several times as many: slower,

57
00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.450
costlier, and limits come sooner.

58
00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:34.970
With less text online in a

59
00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.170
language, answers are often weaker.

60
00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.450
In the app, type any sentence and watch it

61
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.990
split into coloured tokens, with a count.

62
00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.880
Try a short word, a long

63
00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.700
word, then add a comma.

64
00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.620
Which words break into the most pieces?

65
00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:53.510
Your turn.

66
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.820
Same question, new chat, and I got

67
00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.540
two different rain stories.

68
00:02:59.430 --> 00:02:59.970
Why?

69
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.720
It picks likely tokens, with a

70
00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.520
little chance, so the same

71
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.280
question can get a new story.

72
00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.810
Remember: text becomes tokens, and it

73
00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:13.110
predicts one likely token at a time.

74
00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.340
Fluent isn’t proof.

75
00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:18.550
And some languages cost more tokens.

76
00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:21.450
Try it in class tomorrow:

77
00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:23.450
play “Human chatbot”.

78
00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.800
Pupils build a sentence, one word each,

79
00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.280
each choosing a likely next word.

80
00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.290
Then read it out: does it sound right?

81
00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:33.790
Is it true?

82
00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.220
How would we check?
